A 60-year-old woman presents to your clinic
for evaluation. She has the history of a recent
transient ischemic attack (TIA), and
was diagnosed with atrial fibrillation (AF)
3 months ago. Other significant points in her
history are cigarette smoking and
hypercholesterolemia. A Duplex Doppler
sonography of the carotid arteries has been
performed which shows 40% stenosis of the
left and 50% stenosis of the right carotid
arteries. On examination, she is found to
have a blood pressure of 180/110 mmHg on
