Font Siz: A A A
A 30-year-old woman presents to your GP
clinic with complaint of headaches 3 to 5 days
before her periods. The headache often
starts from the back of her head (occipital
area) and radiates to her left temple, and is
aggravated by walking or other types of
physical activity. She denies visual, auditory,
or taste disturbances, but mentions that
during attacks she cannot tolerate noisy or
crowded places. According to her, every
episode of headache lasts from two to
several hours. Physical examination, including
a full neurological exam is inconclusive.
